$article=['date'=>'2026-12-07','slug'=>'adhd-and-autism-together','tag'=>'ADHD','category'=>'ADHD','breadcrumb'=>'ADHD and Autism Together','meta_title'=>'ADHD and Autism Together: A Practical Guide for Parents','description'=>'Can a child have ADHD and autism together? Learn how overlapping traits are assessed and why support should address each child&rsquo;s individual needs today.','h1'=>'ADHD and Autism Together: What Parents Need to Know When Both Are on the Table','dek'=>'One diagnosis does not cancel the other, and the same visible behaviour can arise for different reasons.','body'=><<<'HTML'
<p class="text-lg text-gray-700 leading-relaxed font-light">A child can have ADHD and autism spectrum disorder together. Modern diagnostic criteria allow both diagnoses, but overlap in attention, regulation, social difficulty, sensory needs and executive functioning means assessment must examine the pattern carefully.</p><h2>Where traits can overlap</h2><p>Both profiles may include difficulty switching tasks, intense focus, movement, impulsive interaction, emotional dysregulation or friendship problems. The reason matters. A child may miss a social cue through inattention, difficulty interpreting it, or both.</p><h2>What a careful assessment examines</h2><p>Clinicians consider early development, social communication, restricted or repetitive patterns, attention and impulse control across settings, sensory experiences, school information and alternative explanations. No single questionnaire settles the distinction.</p><h2>How co-occurrence changes support</h2><p>A strategy that reduces distraction may not address uncertainty or sensory overload. A predictable routine may support autism-related needs while external reminders and task breakdown support ADHD-related executive difficulty. Plans should follow functioning rather than forcing every need under one label.</p><h2>What about treatment?</h2><p>Support may include parent guidance, school adjustments, psychological approaches, speech-language or occupational input and medical consultation. Medication decisions belong with a qualified prescriber and should consider the individual child, benefits, adverse effects and coexisting conditions.</p><h2>Start with the current barrier</h2><p>Ask what prevents participation today: sleep, communication, transitions, attention, anxiety, sensory load or learning. Addressing that barrier can begin while diagnostic questions are still being clarified. Learn about <a href="/adhd/adhd-and-autism">ADHD and autism together</a>.</p>
HTML,'faqs'=>[['Can ADHD and autism be diagnosed together?','Yes. Current diagnostic criteria permit both diagnoses when the child independently meets requirements for each and the combined formulation best explains their developmental pattern.'],['Can ADHD hide autism?','It can complicate recognition. Impulsivity or inattention may attract attention while social-communication, sensory or repetitive patterns are overlooked or attributed entirely to ADHD.'],['Does having both change medication decisions?','It may. A qualified prescriber should consider the child&rsquo;s complete profile, target symptoms, physical health, coexisting conditions, expected benefits and possible adverse effects.'],['Which condition should be treated first?','Support should prioritise the needs causing greatest impairment or risk. Plans often address several areas together and should be reviewed as the child develops.']],
